#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:19
msgid "Bluetooth OLED"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:29
msgid "This project uses an Android app created with MIT App Inventor to send messages over Bluetooth to an Arduino device. The Arduino, upon receiving the messages, displays them on an OLED screen. The Android app, designed with a user-friendly interface, allows users to input messages and send them with the press of a button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:31
msgid "The Android application will be constructed utilizing a complimentary web-based platform known as |link_appinventor|. The project presents an excellent opportunity to gain familiarity with the interfacing of an Arduino with a smartphone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:35
msgid "1. Build the Circuit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:40
msgid ":ref:`cpn_uno`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:41
msgid ":ref:`cpn_jdy31`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:42
msgid ":ref:`cpn_olde`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:46
msgid "2. Create the Android App"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:48
msgid "The Android application will be developed using a free web application known as |link_appinventor|. MIT App Inventor serves as an excellent starting point for Android development, owing to its intuitive drag-and-drop features allowing for the creation of simplistic applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:52
msgid "Now, let's begin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:54
msgid "Go to |link_appinventor_login|, and click \"online tool\" to login. You will require a Google account to register with MIT App Inventor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:60
msgid "After logging in, navigate to **Projects** -> **Import project (.aia) from my computer**. Subsequently, upload the ``oled.aia`` file located in the path ``ultimate-sensor-kit\\code\\iot_project_esp01\\bluetooth\\07-Bluetooth_oled``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:62
msgid "You can also directly download here: :download:`oled.aia</_static/other/oled.aia>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:67
msgid "Upon uploading the ``.aia`` file, you will see the application on the MIT App Inventor software. This is a pre-configured template. You can modify this template after you have familiarized yourself with MIT App Inventor through the following steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:69
msgid "In MIT App Inventor, you have 2 primary sections: the **Designer** and the **Blocks**. You can switch between these two sections in the upper right corner of the page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:73
msgid "The **Designer** allows you to add buttons, text, screens, and modify the overall aesthetic of your application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:77
msgid "Next, there's the **Blocks** section. This section lets you craft custom functionalities for your app, allowing you to program each component on the app's GUI to achieve desired features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:81
msgid "To install the application on a smartphone, navigate to the **Build** tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:85
msgid "You can generate a ``.apk`` file. After selecting this option, a page will appear allowing you to choose between downloading a ``.apk`` file or scanning a QR code for installation. Follow the installation guide to complete the application installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:87
msgid "You also download our pre-compiled APK here: :download:`piano.apk</_static/other/piano.apk>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:89
msgid "If you wish to upload this app to Google Play or another app marketplace, you can generate a ``.aab`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:93
msgid "3. Upload the Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:95
msgid "Open the ``07-Bluetooth_oled.ino`` file under the path of ``ultimate-sensor-kit\\code\\iot_project_esp01\\bluetooth\\07-Bluetooth_oled``, or copy this code into **Arduino IDE**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:98
msgid "To install the library, use the Arduino Library Manager and search for **\"Adafruit SSD1306\"** and **\"Adafruit GFX\"** and install it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:104
msgid "After selecting the correct board and port, click the **Upload** button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:106
msgid "Open the Serial monitor(set baudrate to **9600**) to view debug messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:109
msgid "4. App and Bluetooth module Connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:111
msgid "Ensure that the application created earlier is installed on your smartphone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:113
msgid "Initially, turn on **Bluetooth** on your smartphone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:119
msgid "Navigate to the **Bluetooth settings** on your smartphone and look for names like **JDY-31-SPP**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:125
msgid "After clicking it, agree to the **Pair** request in the pop-up window. If prompted for a pairing code, please enter \"1234\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:131
msgid "Now open the newly installed **OLED** APP."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:137
msgid "In the APP, click on **Connect** button to establish a connection between the APP and Bluetooth module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:143
msgid "This page displays a list of all paired Bluetooth devices. Choose the ``xx.xx.xx.xx.xx.xx JDY-31-SPP`` option from the list. The name of each device is listed next to its MAC address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:149
msgid "After a successful connection, the OLED display will switch from showing \"Waiting for connection\" to displaying the MAC address of the connected device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:159
msgid "If you don't see any devices on the page shown above, it could be because this app is not authorized to scan for nearby devices. In such a case, you will need to adjust the settings manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:161
msgid "To access the **APP Info** page, long-press the app icon and select it. Alternatively, if you have another method to reach this page, use that instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:167
msgid "Navigate to the **Permissions** page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:173
msgid "To enable the APP to scan for nearby devices, go to **Nearby devices** and select **Always**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:179
msgid "Now, restart the APP and repeat steps 5 and 6 to successfully connect to Bluetooth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:181
msgid "After a successful connection, you will be redirected to the main page. Enter your desired message in the text box provided and click on the send button to display it on the OLED screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:188
msgid "5. Code explanation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:190
msgid "**Setting Up Bluetooth Communication**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:192
msgid "This section includes the ``SoftwareSerial`` library and sets up the digital pins for Bluetooth communication. The standard ``Serial`` is for debugging while ``bleSerial`` is specifically for Bluetooth communication."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:201
msgid "**Setting Up OLED Display**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:203
msgid "The libraries and constants required to initialize and manage the OLED display are declared here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:217
msgid "**Initialization**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:219
msgid "In the ``setup()`` function, serial communications are initialized. The OLED display is started, and an initial message \"Waiting for connection\" is displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:241
msgid "**Main Loop**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/12-iot_Bluetooth_oled.rst:243
msgid "Within the ``loop()``, the code constantly checks for incoming data from the Bluetooth module. Once a full message (ending with a '#', the APP will automatically add a '#' at the end of the message that the user send) is received, it's displayed on the OLED. Also, the received message is printed to the serial monitor for debugging purposes."
msgstr ""
